  "question": {
    "text": "The total length of a sonometer wire fixed between two bridges is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>110</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. Now, two more bridges are placed to divide the length of the wire in the ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math>. If the tension in the wire is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> and the mass per unit length of the wire is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, then the minimum common frequency with which all the three parts can vibrate, is",
